= ECON 0100 | Exercise B2 | Supply

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  = Solutions

]

_Answers and work are shown in the red blocks._

Pumpkin pasties are produced by many sellers according to the following supply curve:

#mitex(`
P = 2 + \frac{Q_s}{10}
`)

Prices are in galleons and quantity is in pasties.

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  *Plot.* Vertical intercept, $Q_s = 0$: $P = 2$. Below $2$ galleons quantity supplied is zero, so the curve starts there and rises by $1$ galleon for every $10$ pasties. This is a flatter supply curve than the Vignette's, $P = 2 + \frac{1}{2} Q_s$, so the numbers below are bigger.
]

== Q1 | Quantity Supplied

a) What is quantity supplied at 10 galleons? \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  $80$ pasties.

  Price to quantity: plug the price into the supply curve and solve for $Q_s$.

  #mitex(`
  10 = 2 + \tfrac{1}{10} Q_s \rightarrow 8 = \tfrac{1}{10} Q_s \rightarrow Q_s = 80
  `)
]

b) What is marginal cost at 9 pasties? \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  $2.9$ galleons per pasty.

  Quantity to price: the height of the supply curve at $9$ pasties is the marginal cost of the ninth pasty, the least a seller will take for it: $P = 2 + \frac{1}{10} \cdot 9 = 2.9$.
]

== Q2 | Producer Surplus

What is producer surplus at 10 galleons? \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  #grid(
    columns: (1fr, 1fr),
    gutter: 1em,
    [
    PS $= 320$ galleons.

      Producer surplus is the triangle above supply and below the price, from $Q = 0$ out to the $80$ pasties sold in Q1. Its height is the price minus the $2$-galleon intercept and its base is the quantity:

      #mitex(`
      \text{PS} = h \cdot b \cdot \tfrac{1}{2} = (10 - 2) \cdot 80 \cdot \tfrac{1}{2} = 320
      `)

      Revenue at $10$ galleons is the rectangle $10 \cdot 80 = 800$; the $320$ above the curve is surplus and the $480$ below it covers marginal costs.
    ],
    [
      #image("data:image/svg+xml;base64,", width: 100%)
    ],
  )
]

